say what a direct pixel writer was handed

O2BitmapContextCreate already reported whether it answered ok or NULL. The other half was missing:
what the buffer POINTER turned out to be, for a caller that takes it and writes pixels itself.

Money Manager EX does exactly that. It rasterises an SVG with nanosvg, then premultiplies the result
into a buffer it believes wxBitmap::GetRawData returned, and it faulted on the first byte store. The
registers said the destination was the integer 3, not a pointer, and CR2 was 1, which is 3 minus the
instruction offset of 2. Nothing in the port could say whether that 3 had come from here.

With this it can, and the answer is no. Both calls in that run returned real heap pointers:

  CIDER_IMAGESOURCE O2BitmapContextGetData ctx=0x745fe4b9a840 surface=0x745fe4b96930 -> 0x746082137000
  CIDER_IMAGESOURCE O2BitmapContextGetData ctx=0x745fe4b97e20 surface=0x745fe4b97fe0 -> 0x74608212f000

On CIDER_TRACE_IMAGESOURCE, beside the create line already there.

--- a/Onyx2D/O2BitmapContext.m
+++ b/Onyx2D/O2BitmapContext.m
@@ -122,8 +122,18 @@
 
 void *O2BitmapContextGetData(O2ContextRef selfX) {
     O2BitmapContextRef self = (O2BitmapContextRef) selfX;
+    void *bytes = [self->_surface pixelBytes];
 
-    return [self->_surface pixelBytes];
+    /* WHETHER A CALLER THAT WRITES PIXELS DIRECTLY GOT THEM FROM HERE. wxWidgets rasterises an SVG
+     * and premultiplies it into a buffer it believes this returned; when that buffer is not a
+     * pointer at all the fault is eleven frames away and names only the application. Task #212. */
+    if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_IMAGESOURCE") != NULL &&
+        getenv("CIDER_TRACE_IMAGESOURCE")[0] != '\0') {
+        fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_IMAGESOURCE O2BitmapContextGetData ctx=%p surface=%p -> %p\n",
+                selfX, self->_surface, bytes);
+        fflush(stderr);
+    }
+    return bytes;
 }
 
